You have in effect been saying that that {djuno} means

   epistemology x4 convinces x1 that x2 is true of x3 by a certain
       metaphysics, M

What I meant by "correct" - feel free to offer an alternative term
for this if you prefer it - is that

   x1 is "correct" [i.e. in djunoing] if x2 is indeed true of x3 by M

and

   x1 is "incorrect" if x2 is not true of x3 by M

The "true-x2" djuno meaning would be:

   x2 is true of x3 by a certain metaphysics, M, and epistemology x4
   convinces x1 that x2 is true of x3 by M

-- i.e. x1 must be "correct".

